---
.title = "The relay: a peer without a socket",
.date = @date("2026-07-21T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A relay carries a peer's keyed CapTP session over a WebSocket to a peer that has a
socket. It is there for the browser. A browser opens no listening socket and dials
no raw TCP or QUIC connection, so it has no netlayer of its own, and the relay stands
in as the byte carrier. Only bytes cross it
([ADR-0086](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0086-byte-transparent-relay.md)).

## Byte transparency

The relay never parses a frame and never holds a capability. The keyed
`op:start-session` handshake and every capability that follows it run between the
browser and the far peer, and the relay sees only the bytes on the wire. The
session's authority is the keys at its two ends; the relay holds none of it, so it
carries a session without being party to it.

## Reaching the other side

A peer with a socket dials the address it wants. A browser has none, so the relay
reaches the far side two ways.

### Dialing a far peer

The browser names a target and the relay dials it. The rooms relay a browser talks
to opens `ws://relay/relay?target=<locator>` and classifies that locator through the
same `pancake_host_plan_dial` a native dial uses: a `host:port` opens a TCP socket,
an `ocapn://` node opens an iroh channel. The relay then pipes the WebSocket to
whatever it dialed.

[The browser names the peer it wants; the relay dials it over tcp or iroh and pipes the two together. The keyed session runs end to end, and the relay only moves bytes.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-relay-dial.svg'))

The dial policy is the embedding app's. The [rooms demo]($link.page('examples'))
pins its TCP target to the room server, so a classified TCP dial reaches the room
and nothing else. iroh reaches any key-verified node, a wider destination the app
accepts and bounds by capacity and backpressure.

### Cold-dial rendezvous

When both ends are browsers, neither can be dialed. A browser registers its key with
the relay and leaves an inbound socket held there. A second browser, holding an
introduction that names that key, cold-dials it, and the relay bridges the dialer to
the held socket
([ADR-0088](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0088-fully-functioning-browser-peer.md)).
The room that made the introduction carries no part of the session.

[One browser registers and waits; the other redeems an introduction by cold-dialing that identity. The relay bridges the held socket to the dialer, and the keyed session runs end to end between the two browsers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-relay-rendezvous.svg'))

Both shapes run through one byte pump, so the bridge and the dial share their
backpressure and their teardown.

## Bounded resources

A relay carries other peers' sessions, so it bounds what it holds. Backpressure on
both legs caps the bytes in flight: a stalled reader stops the relay reading its
partner, and the stall reaches the sender instead of the middle
(`zig build deno-relay-backpressure-test`). A silent session is reclaimed on an idle
timeout (`zig build deno-relay-idle-test`). An embedding app adds its own ceilings on
the live-session and held-registration counts as admission policy, which the
[rooms demo]($link.page('examples')) sets. The relay counts bytes and sessions, never
messages, because it reads none.

## One contract, every runtime

The relay is a small contract an embedder runs in its own runtime. `@pancake/relay`
is the Deno reference, and Python, Rust, and Racket carry the same one. One harness
runs the same three checks (forwarding, backpressure, idle reclaim) against every
runtime's relay (`zig build bindings-relay`), so a browser peer holds the same keyed
session whichever runtime carries it.

## Not a netlayer

The relay is not a netlayer. pancake ships four of those (TCP, keyed-tcp,
in-process, iroh), and a session dispatches on a
[locator]($link.page('concepts'))'s transport symbol; the relay is none of them, and
nothing dials it. The cold-dial rendezvous does put a `keyed-relay` symbol on a
locator, `ocapn://<key>.keyed-relay`, but that locator names the waiting peer's key,
and the relay stays resident-less (ADR-0088). A peer with its own socket dials a
[transport]($link.page('transports')) directly and never touches a relay.

## See it run

Every claim here is a check step. Each needs `deno`, and the iroh leg also needs
`cargo`:

```sh
zig build bindings-relay        # the cross-runtime matrix: forwarding + backpressure + idle reclaim, every runtime
zig build deno-relay-test       # a browser peer holds a keyed session through the Deno reference relay
zig build bindings-relay-iroh   # the same session, dialing a live iroh endpoint over QUIC through each relay
zig build rooms-web-dm-tabs     # two browser tabs cold-dial each other by relay rendezvous
```

The [rooms]($link.page('examples')) chat and its browser direct messages run over a
real relay. [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers locators, handoffs, and
sturdyrefs, [Transports]($link.page('transports')) sets the netlayers beside each
other, and [the TypeScript binding]($link.page('bindings/ts')) is where the browser
peer lives.
